{
  "teamId": 120,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Washington Nationals entered the season carrying the quiet pride of a franchise that had won it all just two years prior, but cracks in the roster were visible from the opening week. April was a bumpy introduction, with a Pablo Sandoval homer off Tanner Rainey in the first week setting an early tone of bullpen vulnerability, though Kyle Schwarber quickly emerged as a bright spot, homering twice in the month and providing the kind of power the lineup desperately needed. The Nationals finished April at 10-12, treading water but not yet sinking.\n\nMay brought more struggles as the bullpen continued to falter, and the team slid to 12-17 for the month. But then something unexpected happened in June: Washington caught fire. A 19-9 record in June — the best month of the season by a wide margin — was punctuated by a Josh Bell grand slam on June 23rd that showcased what this lineup could do at its peak, with Schwarber, Trea Turner, and Juan Soto all on base. For a fleeting moment, it felt like the Nationals might be legitimate contenders. That illusion, however, was shattered by the trade deadline, when the front office dealt Turner, Schwarber, and several others, signaling a full pivot toward rebuilding.\n\nJuly and August told the real story of what Washington was becoming. The team went 8-19 in July and a brutal 7-21 in August, with the bullpen — Brad Hand, Tanner Rainey, Kyle Finnegan, and others — surrendering key blows night after night. Andrew McCutchen's three-run homer off Brad Hand on July 26th, a play that cost Washington nearly 0.8 win probability, was perhaps the most painful single moment of the collapse. The post-deadline roster was thin, and it showed in every close game that slipped away.\n\nSeptember offered a few silver linings amid the wreckage. Riley Adams and Andrew Stevenson, players fighting for roster spots on a team in transition, provided unexpected heroics — Adams with a clutch double, Stevenson with a walk-off homer and a game-winning single. These moments belonged to a new generation finding its footing, even as veteran relievers continued to bleed leads late in games. The Nationals finished 65-97, their worst record since 2008, closing out the season in Boston with Kyle Finnegan surrendering a Rafael Devers homer as the final chapter of a painful but clarifying year.",
